<!--
  Active-response report (active-responses.log), Linux and Windows.

  WHY. Wazuh's active-response channel is fire-and-forget: the API returns
  as soon as the command is queued, and the script's exit code never comes
  back. soc-agent therefore logged every remediation as "executed" as soon
  as the API had accepted the command. On 2026-08-02, this produced an
  IRIS report claiming 26 successful quarantines on a domain controller,
  while the script had actually REFUSED every single one (paths under
  System32). The analyst read "done" on something that was not done.

  This decoder is the missing feedback loop. AR scripts now write, on
  every exit, a fixed-format line:

    2026/08/02 09:21:11 ar-result: script=win-quarantine-file status=refused
    target="C:\Windows\System32\cmd.exe" reason="protected directory ..."

  The format is identical on the shell side (`ar_result()`) and the
  PowerShell side (`Write-ARResult` in _ar-common.ps1) — one decoder for
  both. `soc_agent.reconcile` then correlates these alerts with the
  `mitigations` table and replaces the "issued" status with what actually
  happened.

  `status` is a closed set: applied | refused | noop | error.

  TWO GOTCHAS, both paid for in round-trips with wazuh-logtest:

  1. `offset="after_prematch"` is only valid if the `prematch` is in THE
     SAME decoder. Split into parent + child, Wazuh refuses to start:
     "ERROR: (2120): Invalid offset value: 'after_prematch'". Hence a
     single decoder rather than the usual parent/child pair.
  2. The default syntax is OS_Regex, which does NOT support negative
     classes. `[^"]*` silently fails there, even though it's essential
     here: a target is a Windows path, with spaces. Hence `type="pcre2"`.
-->
<decoder name="ar-result">
  <prematch>ar-result: script=</prematch>
  <regex type="pcre2" offset="after_prematch">^(\S+) status=(\w+) target="([^"]*)" reason="([^"]*)"</regex>
  <order>ar_script,ar_status,ar_target,ar_reason</order>
</decoder>
